<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turkic peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Turkic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Turkic peoples are a large family of ethnic groups speaking Turkic languages, originating in the steppes of Inner and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> was the first state to bear the Turkic name, and Turkic groups later founded or dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. As soldiers and slave-soldiers they were also central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kara-Khanids were the first Turkic dynasty to make Islam the religion of their state, and their conversion opened the way for the Islamisation of the <b>Turkic peoples</b> of Central Asia, from whom later Muslim Turkic dynasties such as the Seljuks emerged.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Turkic peoples</b> of Central Asia adopted Islam from the 10th century and carried it into Anatolia and India.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…as one of the world’s oldest religions, and since the dissolution of the Soviet Union it has spread among intellectuals in Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tatarstan and Bashkortostan and is undergoing an organised revival in Siberia; Nursultan Nazarbayev and Askar Akayev called it the “natural” religion of the <b>Turkic peoples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Other <b>Turkic peoples</b> moved westwards across the steppe: the Oghuz, among whom the Seljuks rose to rule Iran and Anatolia in the 11th century, and the Kipchaks of the western steppe.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution, Muslim congresses in Kazan and Ufa debated autonomy for the <b>Turkic peoples</b> of the Volga and Urals.</blockquote><a href="/state/idel-ural-state">Idel-Ural State</a></li><li><blockquote>Their remnants merged into later <b>Turkic peoples</b> of the Zhetysu region.</blockquote><a href="/state/wusun">Wusun</a></li></ul></section>
